---
// WEBSITE.md §5.8: the mission and values edited for a public audience, the
// privacy position, and the data sources with their honest confidence - the
// ODbL line here is a legal obligation, not a courtesy. The full values
// document lives in the repository and is linked rather than duplicated;
// this page is the reading of it a hiker or a journalist needs.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

const title = 'About OurHike'
const description =
  'What OurHike is, the values it is built against, where its data comes from, and why position never leaves your phone.'

const values = [
  {
    name: 'Hike your own hike',
    line: 'Accurate information and good tools, then we get out of the way. No streaks, no scores, no "right" way to hike.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Built by the community that built the trail',
    line: 'Volunteers cut the first mile of the AT in 1923 and have maintained it since. Conditions and corrections come from hikers and maintainers on the ground.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Open by default',
    line: 'Open source, open formats. If OurHike ever disappears, any club can fork it and keep going — that is designed in, not hoped for.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Trustworthy above all',
    line: 'People decide which way at a junction with this. Honesty about uncertainty beats polish, every time it is a choice.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Free for every hiker',
    line: 'Conditions, closures, hazards and the map itself stay free, forever. Safety never sits behind a paywall.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Belongs to the trails, not the platform',
    line: 'Money and data follow the trail they came from, back to the people who maintain it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Built to be inherited',
    line: 'Every decision is tested against one question: could another trail club pick this up with minimal friction?',
  },
  {
    name: 'Sustainable, not just launched',
    line: 'Boring, well-supported technology that survives its builders losing weekends.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Be magical',
    line: 'And after all of that — the moment the map draws in a dead zone should still feel like a small miracle.',
  },
]
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <main class="wrap">
    <header class="section">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>What this is</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        OurHike is a free, open-source, offline-first map of the Appalachian
        Trail — built by volunteers, for the places where a map that needs
        signal is no map at all. It exists because the tools trail communities
        depend on keep getting bought, closed, or shut down, and the answer to
        that is a tool the community can own outright: source open, data
        formats open, and the whole thing designed to be handed to a trail
        club and survive.
      </p>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: 12px">
        OurHike is an independent project. It is not affiliated with or
        endorsed by the Appalachian Trail Conservancy or any trail club — it
        exists to serve them, and everything about how it is run is written
        down in the open.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section--tight">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>The nine values</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom: 20px">
        Every design decision in this project is argued against nine written
        values. The short reading of each — the
        <a href="https://github.com/OurHike/OurHike/blob/main/OurHikeValues.md"
          >full document</a
        > argues them properly:
      </p>
      <ol class="values">
        {
          values.map((value) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{value.name}.</strong> {value.line}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight prose">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Privacy: position never leaves the phone</h2>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 12px">
        Where you are on the trail is the most sensitive thing this app could
        know, and the design answer is that it never leaves the device. The
        map, your position, your plans and your trip log live in the phone's
        own storage. No analytics follow you down the trail, and no background
        location runs when the app is closed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some things you can choose to send — a trail-condition report, a shared
        photo — and sending is always your explicit act, never a background
        one. The <a href="/Privacy/">privacy policy</a> is written in plain
        language rather than lawyer's.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section--tight prose" style="padding-bottom: 24px">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 12px">
        The trail centerline and facilities come from five organizations'
        published GIS layers: the Appalachian Trail Conservancy, the New
        York-New Jersey Trail Conference, NY State Parks, Mohonk Preserve, and
        NYS DEC. The topo background is USGS US Topo; elevation is sampled
        from USGS 3DEP survey data. Water sources draw on © OpenStreetMap
        contributors (ODbL) and the National Hydrography Dataset.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those sources are not equally certain, and the app says so instead of
        flattening them: an ATC-surveyed shelter and a crowd-mapped spring are
        labelled as what they are, and anything unverified is marked
        unverified where you read it. A confidently wrong answer is more
        dangerous than an honest unknown — that rule is written into the
        project and it outranks looking finished.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .values {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 22px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    max-width: var(--container-narrow);
  }

  .values li {
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--fg-2);
    padding-left: 6px;
  }

  .values li strong {
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }

  .values li::marker {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--brand-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
